### What is an idempotent producer?

The idempotent producer solves a narrow but critical problem. When a producer sends a record and the broker writes it but the acknowledgement is lost on the network, the producer retries. Without idempotence, that retry becomes a duplicate. With idempotence enabled, the broker assigns the producer a unique producer id and tracks a sequence number per partition. If it sees a record whose sequence it has already written, it acknowledges it again but does not write it twice.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

public class IdempotentProducerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5);

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001",
                "{\"amount\":50000,\"type\":\"DEBIT\"}"));
            System.out.println("Idempotent producer: retry duplicates are dropped");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotent producer: retry duplicates are dropped
```

### What is a transactional producer?

A transactional producer is a superset of the idempotent producer. It requires a `transactional.id`, which lets Kafka fence out a previous instance of the same producer so a zombie cannot keep writing. You call `initTransactions()` once, then wrap work in `beginTransaction()`, sends, and `commitTransaction()` or `abortTransaction()`.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

public class TransactionalProducerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.TRANSACTIONAL_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-tx-1");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");

        KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);
        producer.initTransactions();

        try {
            producer.beginTransaction();
            // Debit from SAV2001
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("debits", "SAV2001",
                "{\"amount\":50000}"));
            // Credit to SAV3045
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("credits", "SAV3045",
                "{\"amount\":50000}"));
            producer.commitTransaction();
            System.out.println("Both debit and credit committed atomically");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            producer.abortTransaction();
            System.out.println("Transaction aborted: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            producer.close();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Both debit and credit committed atomically
```

### How do the two compare? (comparison table)

| Feature | Idempotent Producer | Transactional Producer |
|---|---|---|
| Deduplication | Yes, per partition per session | Yes (inherits idempotence) |
| Scope | One partition, one session | Multiple partitions and topics |
| Atomicity | No | Yes, all-or-nothing |
| Zombie fencing | No (producer id changes on restart) | Yes (epoch bumping via transactional.id) |
| Offset commit | Separate | Can be inside the transaction |
| Performance cost | Minimal | Higher (two-phase commit) |
| Config | enable.idempotence=true | transactional.id + enable.idempotence |

### How does sendOffsetsToTransaction work?

The crucial capability of a transactional producer is `sendOffsetsToTransaction`, which commits the consumer offsets inside the same transaction as the output records. This makes the consume-process-produce loop exactly-once.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.OffsetAndMetadata;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class ExactlyOnceLoop {
    public static void processLoop(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer,
                                    KafkaProducer<String, String> producer) {
        producer.initTransactions();
        while (true) {
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
            if (records.isEmpty()) continue;

            producer.beginTransaction();
            records.forEach(record -> {
                // Process and produce output
                producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("settlements", record.key(),
                    "{\"settled\":true}"));
            });

            // Commit offsets INSIDE the transaction
            Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> offsets = new HashMap<>();
            records.partitions().forEach(tp -> {
                long lastOffset = records.records(tp)
                    .get(records.records(tp).size() - 1).offset();
                offsets.put(tp, new OffsetAndMetadata(lastOffset + 1));
            });
            producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, consumer.groupMetadata());
            producer.commitTransaction();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the consumer participate?

Consumers participate through `isolation.level`. With `read_committed`, a consumer only sees records from committed transactions. With the default `read_uncommitted`, it sees everything immediately.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import java.util.Properties;

public class ReadCommittedConsumer {
    public static Properties config() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "ledger-service");
        props.put("isolation.level", "read_committed");
        props.put("enable.auto.commit", "false");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("read_committed: only sees committed transaction records");
        System.out.println("read_uncommitted: sees all records immediately");
    }
}
```

```text
read_committed: only sees committed transaction records
read_uncommitted: sees all records immediately
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class IdempotentVsTransactionalMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: thinking idempotence prevents all duplicates
        System.out.println("WRONG: idempotence prevents application-level resends");
        System.out.println("  Reality: it only prevents RETRY duplicates within one session");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: using transactions everywhere
        System.out.println("WRONG: wrapping every produce in a transaction");
        System.out.println("  Reality: transactions add latency and coordinator overhead");
        System.out.println("  Use only where atomicity is a hard business requirement");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: forgetting read_committed on consumers
        System.out.println("WRONG: transactional producer + read_uncommitted consumer");
        System.out.println("  Reality: consumer sees aborted records too!");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: idempotence prevents application-level resends
  Reality: it only prevents RETRY duplicates within one session

WRONG: wrapping every produce in a transaction
  Reality: transactions add latency and coordinator overhead
  Use only where atomicity is a hard business requirement

WRONG: transactional producer + read_uncommitted consumer
  Reality: consumer sees aborted records too!
```

### When should you use each?

- **Idempotent producer**: always on (it is the default). It removes retry duplicates for free.
- **Transactional producer**: when you need atomic writes across multiple partitions or topics, or exactly-once read-process-write.
- In banking: idempotence for every producer. Transactions for money movement spanning a debit topic and a credit topic, so consumers never see a lonely debit.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Idempotence is cheap and should essentially always be enabled. Transactions add a two-phase commit through a transaction coordinator, extra latency, and transaction markers in the log, so reserve them for hard atomicity requirements. In Spring Kafka, setting a `transactionIdPrefix` on the producer factory turns the `KafkaTemplate` transactional. External side effects (database writes) are not covered by Kafka transactions, so make those idempotent or use the outbox pattern.
